## [2.3.0] — Hero rebuilt against the reference sites

The right-hand box read as a generic template block: eyebrow, title,
paragraph, three check-bullets, text link. Looked at both reference heroes and
neither uses that pattern.

- **bestfiberinternet.us** — centred single column, no side card at all. The
  ZIP checker is the focal element in a raised panel, with reassurance
  microcopy beneath it and inclusions as a horizontal check-strip below.
- **kineticfiber.us** — inset rounded panel, copy left with an *inline price
  anchor* ("fiber plans start at $19.99/mo"), photo right with a bold circular
  offer badge overlapping it, then a check-strip below.

The shared language is: inclusions run horizontally, the offer is a bold
graphic element, and the price is anchored inline with the copy. None of that
was true of the old card.

### What changed

- **The card is gone.** In its place, a circular offer medallion in brand red
  with an offset outer ring — eyebrow, product, a large "On us", and the
  qualifier. Short lines only, because a circle has no room for prose. The
  "Call to order" button now sits under it.
- **Inline price anchor added** to the copy column, rendered through the
  existing `PriceLockup` and fed by `entryInternetPlan()`, which picks the
  cheapest published fiber plan. The hero's "from" price therefore cannot
  drift away from the plan grid below it.
- **Inclusions moved to a horizontal check-strip** at the foot of the hero,
  replacing the speed marquee that used to sit there. The ticker requirement is
  still met by the trust marquee in the Offers section.
- **Reassurance microcopy** under the ZIP field: free to check, no obligation.
- **Stat strip removed from the hero.** Its three figures — 15,000 miles, 80%,
  2.5 Gbps — are already metric cards in the Why GCI section, so the hero was
  repeating itself and had grown too tall.
- `heroPromos` and `speedMarquee` deleted from `lib/content.ts` rather than
  left behind as dead data that would read as live to the next editor.

---

## [2.2.0] — Icon set

New `components/ui/Icon.tsx`: 19 inline glyphs sharing one grammar — 24x24 box,
no fill, 1.8 stroke, round caps and joins, inheriting `currentColor`. Inline
rather than an icon package, because this is nineteen glyphs and a dependency
would ship several hundred plus a runtime to choose between them.

Icon choice lives in `lib/content.ts`, not in components: `ServiceSection`,
`SpecialOffer` and `FeatureItem` each gained a typed `icon` field, so swapping
a glyph is a one-line data edit and TypeScript rejects a name that does not
exist.

### Placement

| Where | Icon | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Plan card feature bullets | `check` | Replaced plain dots — a tick reads as "included", a dot does not |
| Plan card speed stats | `download` / `upload` | Direction was carried by the word alone |
| Plan card badge row | Service glyph in a tinted circle | Visual anchor on an otherwise text-only card |
| Section eyebrows | Service glyph | Wayfinding between the four lines |
| Why GCI cards | Per-card glyph in a tinted circle | Balances the large metric on the left |
| Offer cards | Category glyph | Distinguishes device, bundle, tablet and accessory offers at a glance |

### Two things worth noting

- **The first `fiber` glyph was redrawn.** As a branching strand it turned to
  mush at 16px on the plan cards. Rewritten as signal arcs radiating from a
  node, which survives the size.
- **The plan-card service glyph is decoration, not information** — every card
  within a section shares its service line. It is kept because a text-only
  card needs a visual anchor, but it is tinted back so it does not compete
  with the badge beside it.

No brand logos are drawn. The offer cards for Apple and Samsung promotions use
generic device glyphs; rendering those companies' marks would be using their
trademarks as iconography.

