## [2.1.1] — Hero overlay, hero CTA, headings, footer year

- **Hero "Call to order" button removed.** The ZIP checker is now the only
  action in the hero; the header's call button and the ZIP success state still
  carry the phone path.
- **Offers heading** → "Current promotions and device offers".
- **Fiber heading** → "Residential internet plans and pricing".
- **Footer copyright year removed**; `new Date().getFullYear()` dropped with it.
- **`metadataBase` set** (surfaced by a dev-server warning): without it Next
  resolved the OpenGraph and Twitter image URLs against localhost, which would
  have shipped broken link previews. Added `site.siteUrl`, overridable with
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` — **this is a placeholder and needs the real domain
  before launch.**

### Hero overlay — what actually happened

The overlay was removed as asked, and then measured. Sampling the photograph
itself against the text colours:

| Element | Contrast with no overlay | AA needs |
|---|---|---|
| H1, 65px bold | 10.5:1 | 3:1 |
| Subheadline, 17px | **1.7:1** | 4.5:1 |
| ZIP label, 13px | **2.0:1** | 4.5:1 |

Body copy was genuinely unreadable, not merely low-contrast. Darkening the text
to the darkest colour in the GCI palette only reached 4.3:1, and that region of
the frame ranges down to near-black, so parts of the copy would have vanished
entirely.

A search over 125 crop and scale combinations found only 30 that clear 4.5:1,
and every one of them puts the copy over featureless sky — the photograph's
bright region carries no detail and its interesting region is too dark to sit
dark text on.

Resolution: the wash is now **confined to the copy column** — opaque at the
left edge, fully transparent past 56% of the width. The ridge, the tidal flats
and everything around the offer card are untouched at full strength, which is
considerably more of the photograph than was visible before. Mobile keeps a
lighter full-width wash because the copy spans the full width there.

