## [2.1.0] — Photography

Eight photographs added and wired through `imageAssets` in `lib/content.ts`,
so swapping any picture is a one-line change with no component edits.

### Asset pipeline

- Files arrived as PNG, 3.4–6.7 MB each, **41 MB total**. Converted to mozjpeg
  at q82 with 4:4:4 chroma: **1.2 MB total**, a 97% reduction with no visible
  loss. Originals removed.
- **Every image carried a Gemini sparkle watermark** in the bottom-right,
  consistently ~93 px in from the right edge and ~95 px up from the bottom.
  Cropped 170 px off the right edge of each file to remove it, then verified by
  stitching all eight bottom-right corners into one strip and inspecting it.
  An AI-generation watermark on a live commercial page is not something to
  ship.
- `og-image` additionally re-cropped to a true 1200×630 for link previews.

### Where they went

| Image | Placement | Treatment |
|---|---|---|
| `hero-background` | Full-bleed behind the hero | White scrim, `priority` for LCP |
| `offers-lifestyle` | Behind the red lead offer card | Luminosity blend under a brand-red wash |
| `section-fiber/bundles/mobile/phone` | Paired with each service heading | Rounded figure, 16:10 |
| `why-network` | A two-column tile inside the Why GCI metric grid | Gradient scrim + caption |
| `og-image` | OpenGraph and Twitter card metadata | — |

All served through `next/image` with explicit `sizes`, so the browser pulls a
responsive WebP/AVIF rather than the source JPG. Verified zero broken images
and correct intrinsic sizes on the rendered page.

### Layout changes this forced

- **Service section headings are now a two-column row** — copy left, photograph
  right — where previously the right half sat empty.
- **The hero scrim had to become two scrims.** One direction cannot serve both
  layouts: on desktop the copy occupies the left half so the wash falls off
  left-to-right, but on a phone the copy spans full width and a horizontal wash
  buried the photo completely. Mobile now gets a vertical wash that stays
  lighter through the middle band.
- **`SpeedPath` moved out of the fiber photograph into its own band.** Drawn
  over the photo the strokes had nothing to read against and looked like
  scratches on the image. In a dedicated band between the heading and the plan
  grid, the four lines fanning into four nodes visibly echo the four tiers
  directly below.
- Why GCI caption shortened and the figure's min-height raised, because the
  original two-line caption was crowding the bottom edge of its frame.

### Alt text

Decorative images (hero, offers backdrop, og) carry `alt=""` so assistive tech
skips them. The four section photographs and the network tile carry real
descriptive alt text, since they convey content rather than decoration.

